An \emph{$(n, \boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}, g)$-rental game}
is defined by a length $n \geq 0$, which is the timeframe over which the game is played;
a sequence of distributions
of agent valuations,
$\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}} = \mathcal{D}_1,\ldots,\mathcal{D}_n$ supported over $\mathcal{V}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{\geq0}$, one for each day; 
and a \emph{reward function} $g$,
whose cumulative value over time the designer aims to maximize.

The payment schedule associated with all bids in an interval $\mathcal{I}_i$
is the same;
we denote by $p_i$ the total payment for a bid in $\mathcal{I}_i$.

An $(n,\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}},g)\textnormal{-over-time cost function}$ is defined as:
\begin{align*}
    c^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}}_{n,g}(x)
    &=
    \begin{cases}
        R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}_{\text{last }n-1},g}_{n-1}-R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}_{\text{last }n-x},g}_{n-x},&\text{if }x\geq 1\\
        0,&\text{otherwise}
    \end{cases} 
    \qquad
    =
    \qquad
    R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}_{\text{last }n-1}},g}_{n-1}-R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}_{\text{last }n-\max\{x,1\}},g}_{n-\max\{x,1\}},
\end{align*}
where $R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}},g}_m$ denotes the expected reward of an optimal $(m,\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}},g)$-rental.
\label{def:cost-function}

\begin{align*}
    c^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}}_{n,g}(x)
    &=
    \begin{cases}
        R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}_{\text{last }n-1},g}_{n-1}-R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}_{\text{last }n-x},g}_{n-x},&\text{if }x\geq 1\\
        0,&\text{otherwise}
    \end{cases} 
    \qquad
    =
    \qquad
    R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}_{\text{last }n-1}},g}_{n-1}-R^{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{D}}_{\text{last }n-\max\{x,1\}},g}_{n-\max\{x,1\}},
\end{align*}

    \label{prop:benefit_swap}






Consider a (not necessarily truthful) stagewise auction $\auc{A}$,
let $w < v$ be valuations,
    and let $b, b'$ be two bids such that $\x{b} > \x{b'}$.
    Then the benefit that agent $w$
    derives by switching 
    from bid $b$ to bid $b'$

    exceeds that of agent $v$,
    that is,
        $\util{w}{b'} - \util{w}{b}
        >
        \util{v}{b'} - \util{v}{b}
        $.

    For any valuation $z$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \util{z}{b'} - \util{z}{b}
        =
        \left( \x{b'} - \x{b} \right) \cdot z - \left( \p{b'} - \p{b} \right).
    \end{equation*}
This is strictly 

decreasing
in $z$, and the claim follows.

\begin{equation*}
        \util{z}{b'} - \util{z}{b}
        =
        \left( \x{b'} - \x{b} \right) \cdot z - \left( \p{b'} - \p{b} \right).
    \end{equation*}

A stagewise auction $\auc{A}$ is called \textnormal{fixed-rate\xspace}
if for every bid $b$,
there exists a price $p$
such that $\auc{A}[b] = (p,\ldots,p)$,
meaning all units carry the same price $p$.

\label{def:allocation-monotonicity}
    A SWAC\xspace\ is:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textnormal{Strongly-Allocation-Monotone\xspace} if for all $w,v\in \mathcal{V}$ such that $w<v$, it holds that $\x{w}\leq \x{v}$.
        \item \textnormal{Allocation-Monotone\xspace} if there is a set $\mathcal{S}$ with $\Pr_{\mathcal{D}}[\mathcal{S}]=0$, and for all $w,v\in\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{S}$, if $w<v$ then $\x{w}\leq \x{v}$.
        \item \textnormal{Weakly-Allocation-Monotone\xspace} if there is a set $\mathcal{S}$ with $\Pr_{\mathcal{D}}[\mathcal{S}]=0$ such that for all $w,v\in\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{S}$, if $w<v$ and $\trew{w}\neq \trew{v}$ then $\x{w}\leq \x{v}$.
    \end{itemize}

\label{lemma:welfare-like}
    For a welfare-like reward function:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item There exists a fixed-rate\xspace\ and strongly-allocation-monotone\xspace\ optimal SWAC\xspace.\label{item:first}
        \item All truthful optimal SWACs\xspace\ are weakly-allocation-monotone\xspace. \label{item:second}
        \item If either the reward or the cost function are strictly increasing, all truthful SWACs\xspace\ are allocation-monotone\xspace.\label{item:third}
        \item All truthful optimal SWACs\xspace\ are reward-monotone\xspace.\label{item:fourth}
    \end{enumerate}

Let $g$ be a reward function matching the conditions of the lemma. Since $g$ is payment-independent, in this proof we abuse notation and write $g(v)$ instead of $g(v,\cdot)$.

Consider an agent $v\in\mathcal{V}$. Since the designer's reward depends only on the number of units sold to the agent, an allocation that would maximize the designer's reward is in
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{X}_v=\argmax{x\in\sqrrange{n}}\set{x\cdot g(v)-c(x)}.
\end{align}

Note that we don't yet know this allocation is implementable, meaning that we don't know we can achieve this allocation by a truthful auction. 

For any valuations $w<v$ and allocations $x_w\in\mathcal{X}_w$ and $x_v\in\mathcal{X}_v$, it holds that:
\begin{align}
    &\trew{w}=x_w\cdot g(w)-c(x_w)\geq x_v\cdot g(w)-c(x_v)\label{eq:2}\\
    &\trew{v}= x_v\cdot g(v)-c(x_v)\geq x_w\cdot g(v)-c(x_w)\label{eq:1}
\end{align}

Rearranging the above equations, we get that 
\begin{align}
    (x_w-x_v)g(w)\geq c(x_w)-c(x_v) \geq (x_w-x_v)g(v)\label{eq:7}.
\end{align}

Suppose $x_w>x_v$. Since $g$ is non-decreasing in the valuation, Eq.~(\ref{eq:7}) yields $g(w)=g(v)$. 

Define an allocation rule $\alloc'(v)=\min\mathcal{X}_v$, where we choose the largest allocation that maximizes the designer's reward for agent $v$. If $\alloc'(w)>\alloc'(v)$ we would have $g(w)=g(v)$, therefore $\mathcal{X}_w=\mathcal{X}_v$ and in particular $\alloc'(w)=\alloc'(v)$, which is a contradiction. Thus $\alloc'$ is a monotone (weakly) increasing allocation rule, and due to Myerson it can be implemented as a truthful fixed-rate\xspace SWAC\xspace; 
given an agent, we ask them to reveal their bid, then sell $\alloc'(v)$ units at the total price of the unique payment rule, such that the payments are fixed-rate\xspace over all days. Since it is a fixed-rate\xspace SWAC\xspace, stagewise-IR\xspace has no affect here and thus Myerson guarantees that this is truthful, and this proves point~\ref{item:first} from the theorem.

Let $\auc{A}$ be an optimal and truthful $(n,\mathcal{D},g,c)$-stagewise auction.  Define $\mathcal{S}=\sett{v\in\mathcal{V}}{\x{v}\notin\mathcal{X}_v}$. For all $v\in\mathcal{S}$ we know that $\trew[\auc{A}]{v}$ is less than the designer's reward from $v$ in an optimal auction, and for all $v\notin\mathcal{S}$ we know $\trew[\auc{A}]{v}$ is as high as possible. Thus since $\auc{A}$ is optimal we know $\Pr[\mathcal{S}]=0$. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item We will prove that $\auc{A}$ is weakly-allocation-monotone\xspace, for point~\ref{item:second} of the proof. Let $w,v\in\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{S}$ such that $w<v$ and $\trew[\auc{A}]{w}\neq \trew[\auc{A}]{v}$. We need to prove $\x{w}\leq\x{v}$.

Suppose $\x{w}>\x{v}$. In this case, as we proved above, $g(w)=g(v)$. Thus, since $\trew[\auc{A}]{w}\neq \trew[\auc{A}]{v}$, the inequality~(\ref{eq:2}) is strict, and subsequently one of the inequalities in eq.~(\ref{eq:7}) is also strict, which is a contradiction to $g(w)=g(v)$ and completes the proof of point~\ref{item:second}.
\item 
We now prove specific cases in which $\auc{A}$ is allocation-monotone\xspace for point~\ref{item:third}. Suppose $w,v\in\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{S}$ but $\trew[\auc{A}]{w}=\trew[\auc{A}]{v}$ (the case of inequality is complete, due to point~\ref{item:second}). In this case equation~(\ref{eq:1}) yields
\begin{align*}
    \trew[\auc{A}]{w}=\x{w}g(w)-c(\x{w})=\trew[\auc{A}]{v}\geq\x{w}g(v)-c(\x{w}),
\end{align*}
giving either $\x{w}=0$, which is enough for allocation-monotone\xspace, or $g(w)=g(v)$. If $g$ is strictly increasing, we are done, so suppose $c$ is strictly increasing. In this case one of the inequalities from eq.~(\ref{eq:7}) is strict, proving that $\x{w}<\x{v}$, and completing the proof of point~\ref{item:third}.
\item Consider $w,v\in\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{S}$. From equation~(\ref{eq:1}) and due to the monotonicity of $g$, we have 
\begin{align*}
    \trew[\auc{A}]{v}&=\x{v}\cdot g(v)-c(\x{v})
    \geq \x{w}\cdot g(v)-c(\x{w})
    \\&\geq \x{w}\cdot g(w)-c(\x{w})=\trew[\auc{A}]{w}
\end{align*}
and we are done.

\end{itemize}

\begin{align}
        \mathcal{X}_v=\argmax{x\in\sqrrange{n}}\set{x\cdot g(v)-c(x)}.
    \end{align}

\begin{align}
        &\trew{w}=x_w\cdot g(w)-c(x_w)\geq x_v\cdot g(w)-c(x_v)\label{eq:2}\\
        &\trew{v}= x_v\cdot g(v)-c(x_v)\geq x_w\cdot g(v)-c(x_w)\label{eq:1}
    \end{align}

\begin{align}
        (x_w-x_v)g(w)\geq c(x_w)-c(x_v) \geq (x_w-x_v)g(v)\label{eq:7}.
    \end{align}

\begin{align*}
        \trew[\auc{A}]{w}=\x{w}g(w)-c(\x{w})=\trew[\auc{A}]{v}\geq\x{w}g(v)-c(\x{w}),
    \end{align*}

\begin{align*}
        \trew[\auc{A}]{v}&=\x{v}\cdot g(v)-c(\x{v})
        \geq \x{w}\cdot g(v)-c(\x{w})
        \\&\geq \x{w}\cdot g(w)-c(\x{w})=\trew[\auc{A}]{w}
    \end{align*}

\label{claim:sequence_to_supremum}
    Let $\auc{A}$ be a truthful SWAC\xspace with revenue-like reward.

    Let $\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{V}$, and define $B=\sup\set{\trew[\auc{A}]{w}:w\in\mathcal{C}}$.

    There is some sequence $\set{v_i}_{i=1}^{\infty}\subseteq\mathcal{C}$, value ${v^*}\in\textnormal{cl}\mathcal{C}$\footnote{\textnormal{cl}$\mathcal{C}$ denotes the closure of set $\mathcal{C}$} and ${x^*}\in[n]$ for which:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $(v_i)$ is (weakly) decreasing\label{item:1_sequence_to_supremum}
        \item $\brackets{\p{v_i}}$ and $\brackets{\trew[\auc{A}]{v_i}}$ are (weakly) increasing\label{item:2_sequence_to_supremum}
        \item $\x{v_i}={x^*}\ \ \forall i$\label{item:3_sequence_to_supremum}
        \item $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} v_i={v^*}$\label{item:4_sequence_to_supremum}
        \item $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{v_i}=B$\label{item:5_sequence_to_supremum}
    \end{enumerate}

Consider some $\auc{A},\mathcal{C}$ and $B$ as defined in the claim. If there is some $w\in\mathcal{C}$ for which $\trew[\auc{A}]{w}=B$, we can define $v_i=w$ for all $i$, ${v^*}=w$ and ${x^*}=\x{w}$; this sequence fulfills all the required properties.

Suppose $B$ is not reached by any $w\in\mathcal{C}$. Since $B$ is a supremum (and not a maximum), there is a sequence of distinct elements $\set{y_i}_{i=1}^{\infty}\subseteq\mathcal{C}$ such that $\set{\trew[\auc{A}]{y_i}}_{i=1}^\infty$ is strictly increasing, and $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{y_i}=B$. We will find a subsequence of $\brackets{y_i}$ that will match the require properties.

For each allocation $k\in\sqrrange{n}$, define $\set{y_i^k}_{i=1}^\infty\subseteq\set{y_i}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ to be the subsequence of the elements that are allocated $k$ units in $\auc{A}$ (while preserving the original order from $\brackets{y_i}$). The matching sequence $\set{\trew[\auc{A}]{y_i^k}}_{i=1}^\infty$ is a monotone subsequence of $\set{\trew[\auc{A}]{y_i}}_{i=1}^\infty$. All monotone subsequences of a converging sequence also converge, so $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{y_i^k}$ is defined for all $k\in\sqrrange{n}$. Since there are only $n+1$ possible allocations, these subsequences $\brackets{\trew[\auc{A}]{y_i^k}}$ cover the entire sequence $\brackets{\trew[\auc{A}]{y_i}}$, hence $B$ is
\begin{align*}
    B=\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{y_i}=\max\set{\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{y_i^k}\mid k\in\sqrrange{n}}.
\end{align*}

Specifically, there is some allocation ${x^*}$ for which $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{y_i^{x^*}}=B$, so define the sequence $v_i=y_i^{x^*}$ for all $i$, and we will show that it fulfills all the properties from the claim. By the choice of $(v_i)$, for every element $v_i$ in the sequence, $\x{v_i}={x^*}$ and $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{v_i}=B$. Thus we showed that $\brackets{v_i}$ fulfills properties~\ref{item:3_sequence_to_supremum} and~\ref{item:5_sequence_to_supremum}.

Now consider some $v_i$ and $v_{i+1}$. Since $\x{v_i}=\x{v_{i+1}}={x^*}$ but $\trew[\auc{A}]{v_i}<\trew[\auc{A}]{v_{i+1}}$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \trew[\auc{A}]{v_i}={x^*}\cdot g(\p{v_i})-c({x^*})<\trew[\auc{A}]{v_{i+1}}={x^*}\cdot g(\p{v_{i+1}})-c({x^*}).
\end{align*}
Since $g$ is non-decreasing, this proves $\p{v_i}<\p{v_{i+1}}$, thus property~\ref{item:2_sequence_to_supremum} is fulfilled. Additionally, due to Prop.~\ref{prop:decreasing-payments}, $v_i>v_{i+1}$, meaning $(v_i)$ is decreasing, which is property~\ref{item:1_sequence_to_supremum}. Finally, since $(v_i)$ is decreasing and bounded by 0, it converges to some valuation ${v^*}\in\mathbb{R}$, and since $v_i\in\mathcal{C}$ for all $i$, it holds that ${v^*}\in\textnormal{cl}\mathcal{C}$, which fulfills the final property,~\ref{item:4_sequence_to_supremum}.

\begin{align*}
        B=\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{y_i}=\max\set{\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{y_i^k}\mid k\in\sqrrange{n}}.
    \end{align*}

\begin{align*}
        \trew[\auc{A}]{v_i}={x^*}\cdot g(\p{v_i})-c({x^*})<\trew[\auc{A}]{v_{i+1}}={x^*}\cdot g(\p{v_{i+1}})-c({x^*}).
    \end{align*}

\label{claim:rev_create_a_tag}
     Let $\auc{A}$ be a truthful SWAC\xspace with respect to revenue-like reward.
     Let $\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{V}$, and define $B=\sup \trew[\auc{A}]{\mathcal{C}}$. Let ${v^*}$ be the limit of the sequence from Claim~\ref{claim:sequence_to_supremum} over $\mathcal{C}$, with allocation ${x^*}$ for all agents with valuations in the sequence.

     There is a SWAC\xspace $\auc{A}'$ in which for all $w\in\mathcal{V}$:  
     \begin{enumerate}
         \item If ${v^*}\in\mathcal{V}$, then $\trew[\auc{A}']{{v^*}}= B$.\label{item:1_rev_create_a_tag}
         \item If $w<{v^*}$, then either $\trew[\auc{A}']{w}=\trew[\auc{A}]{w}$ or $\trew[\auc{A}']{w}=B$.\label{item:2_rev_create_a_tag}
         \item If $w<{v^*}$ and $\x[\auc{A}]{w}> {x^*}$, then $\trew[\auc{A}']{w}=B$.\label{item:3_rev_create_a_tag}
         \item If $w>{v^*}$, then $\trew[\auc{A}']{w}\geq \trew[\auc{A}]{w}$.\label{item:4_rev_create_a_tag}
         \item If $w>{v^*}$ and $\trew[\auc{A}]{w}<B$, then $\trew[\auc{A}']{w}>\trew[\auc{A}]{w}$.\label{item:5_rev_create_a_tag}
     \end{enumerate}




Let $\auc{A}$ be a truthful SWAC\xspace w.r.t. revenue-like reward $g(v,p)=\alpha p$, let $\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{V}$, and define $B=\sup \trew[\auc{A}]{\mathcal{C}}$. Let $\brackets{v_i}$ be the sequence from Claim~\ref{claim:sequence_to_supremum} with its limit ${v^*}$ and allocation $\x[\auc{A}]{v_i}={x^*}$. 

Define a set $S^\ast\coloneq\sett{v\in\mathcal{V}}{v>{v^*}\textnormal{ and }\trew[\auc{A}]{v}<B}$.
The main effect we want from $\auc{A}'$ is to strictly increase the designer's reward from agents in $S^\ast$, while making sure no other agent yields worse reward.


     To cause agents in $S^*$ to change their bid and give us improved reward,
     we remove $S^*$
     from the bidding space ---
     but that is not enough,
     as agents in $S^*$ may change their bid to a valuation that yields \emph{lower} reward.
     To prevent this,
     we
     must also block off two types of bids:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Bids $w > {v^*}$ that yield lower reward than $B$:
           \begin{equation*}
            \textsc{RemRt}=\sett{w\in\mathcal{V}}{w>{v^*}\textnormal{ but }\trew[\auc{A}]{w}< B}.
        \end{equation*}
        This includes the set $S^*$.
        \item Bids $w < {v^*}$ that receive a higher allocation than ${x^*}$:
        \begin{equation*}
            \textsc{RemLft}=\sett{w\in\mathcal{V}}{w<{v^*}\textnormal{ but }\x[\auc{A}]{w} > {x^*}}.
        \end{equation*}
        These bids must be removed to prevent agents in $\textsc{RemRt}$ (including $S^*$) from bidding values lower than $v^*$, as we show below. 
    \end{enumerate}

   Formally, we define $\auc{A}'$ as follows:

   The bid space of $\auc{A}'$ will be $\brackets{\mathcal{V}\cup\set{{v^*}}}\setminus\brackets{\textsc{RemLft}\cup\textsc{RemRt}}$, and:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For bid ${v^*}$, the designer of $\auc{A}'$ will sell ${x^*}$ units at a total price of $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty}\p[\auc{A}]{v_i}$, with fixed-rate\xspace payments.
        \item For all other bids, $\auc{A}'$ will offer exactly the same allocation and payment schedule as $\auc{A}$ for the same bid.
    \end{itemize}

Note that indeed $\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \p[\auc{A}]{v_i}$ is defined, since the sequence $\set{\p[\auc{A}]{v_i}}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ is increasing and bounded from above: every element $\p[\auc{A}]{v_i}$ is bounded by ${x^*} v_i$ due to IR, therefore the whole sequence is bounded by  ${x^*} v_1$.

In Figure~\ref{figure:remRtLft} we can see an example of such a transformation from $\auc{A}$ to $\auc{A}'$, assuming $v_i={v^*}$ for all $i$, for ease of exposition.

