What is the designer's reward in $\auc{A}'$? Besides for ${v^*}$, agents who don't change their bid between $\auc{A}$ and $\auc{A}'$ still yield the same designer reward, because their outcome is the same. Thus we restrict the analysis to agents who change their bid. 
There are only a few reasons an agent may change their bid: if their bid in $\auc{A}$ was removed; if their bid was changed, in the case of agent ${v^*}$; or if they have a new and better option in $\auc{A}'$ --- this could only be bidding ${v^*}$ as no other bid was added or changed.

Before analyzing these cases, observe that 
\begin{align*}
            \rew[\auc{A}']{\cdot}{{v^*}}&=\alpha{\p[\auc{A}']{{v^*}}}-c\brackets{\x[\auc{A}']{{v^*}}}=\alpha\brackets{\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty}{\p[\auc{A}]{v_i}}}-c({x^*})\\
        &=\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty}\brackets{\alpha\p[\auc{A}]{v_i}-c(\x[\auc{A}]{v_i})}=\lim\limits_{i\rightarrow\infty} \trew[\auc{A}]{v_i}=B.\addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation}\label{eq:reward-at-sup}
\end{align*}

We now analyze each individual case, to show how the reward from each agent changes between $\auc{A}$ and $\auc{A}'$.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Agent ${v^*}$ will be truthful in $\auc{A}'$ (assuming ${v^*}\in\mathcal{V}$), as we show in each case:
    \begin{itemize}
            \item If $\x[\auc{A}']{{v^*}}=\x[\auc{A}]{{v^*}}$ and $\p[\auc{A}']{{v^*}}=\p[\auc{A}]{{v^*}}$: Since $\util[\auc{A}']{{v^*}}{{v^*}}=\util[\auc{A}]{{v^*}}{{v^*}}$, and there's no other bid with an increase in the designer's revenue from $\auc{A}$ to $\auc{A}'$, the tie-breaking does not change and agent ${v^*}$ bids ${v^*}$ in $\auc{A}'$.
        \item Else, let $u$ be the new bid of ${v^*}$ in $\auc{A}'$, and suppose $u\neq {v^*}$. By the definition of $\auc{A}'$,
        \begin{align*}
