We prove this claim by induction on $n$. For $n=1$ it is immediate, so we assume correctness up to $n-1$ and prove for $n\geq 2$.
Let $g,\mathcal{D}$ as defined in the lemma, and let $n>m\geq 1$. By choice of $g$ and $\mathcal{D}$, we can select an optimal and truthful $\brackets{m,\mathcal{D},g,c_m^{\mathcal{D},g}}$-auction $\auc{A}_m$ with $\x[\auc{A}_m]{v}\in\set{1,m}$ for all $v\in\mathcal{V}$. Denote the threshold of $\alloc[\auc{A}_m]$ with $t_m$, and the matching total payments with $p_1,p_m$ such that:
\begin{align*}
 \x[\auc{A}_m]{v}=\begin{cases}
    1,&v<t_m\\
    m,&t_m\leq v
\end{cases}\ \ \ \ \textnormal{and}\ \ \ \ \ \    \p[\auc{A}_m]{v}=\begin{cases}
    p_1,&v<t_m\\
    p_m,&t_m\leq v
\end{cases}.
\end{align*}
Additionally, denote the corresponding filters with $f_m$.

We first make an observation that will aid as in the design of a new auction. Let $v<t_m$. We know that either $v<f_m$ or $mv-p_m\leq v-p_1$. If $mv-p_m\leq v-p_1$ it follows that $v\leq(p_m-p_1)/(m-1)\leq p_m$. So either way, $p_m\geq t_m$.

We design an $n$-auction as follows:
\begin{align*}
    \x[\auc{A}_n]{v}=\begin{cases}
        1,& v<t_m\\
        n,& v\geq t_m
    \end{cases}.
\end{align*}
For bids $v$ such that $\x[\auc{A}_m]{v}= 1$ we keep $\p[\auc{A}_n]{v}=\p[\auc{A}_m]{v}=p_1$. For larger bids, the payment rule is a bit more complicated:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The total payment will be
    \begin{equation}
        \p[\auc{A}_n]{v}=p_n\coloneq\frac{n}{m}p_m
    \end{equation}
    \item The payment for the first day will be the \emph{filter}:
    \begin{equation}
        \f[\auc{A}_n]{v}=f_n\coloneq t_m
    \end{equation}
    That is, the filter is the lowest valuation of agent who is allocated $m$ units in $\auc{A}_m$. Indeed it's possible, as we observed that $t_m\leq p_m\leq \frac{n}{m}p_m$. 
    \item The payment for the subsequent units will be fixed-rate\xspace and amount to the remaining payment due. That is, for units $2,\dots,n$ the payment will be $\frac{p_n-f_n}{n-1}$ each day.
\end{itemize}

We will show truthfulness of $\auc{A}_n$. Consider some $v\in\mathcal{V}$.
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $v<t_m$, the agent can only bid below $t_m=f_n$, which will always result in  the same outcome: 1 unit for payment $p_1$. Hence in this case, agent $v$ is truthful.
    \item If $v\geq t_m$, we have:
    \begin{align*}
        \util[\auc{A}_n]{v}{v}=\x[\auc{A}_n]{v}v-\p[\auc{A}_n]{v}=nv-\frac{n}{m}p_m=\frac{n}{m}\util[\auc{A}_m]{v}{v}\geq \util[\auc{A}_m]{v}{v}.
    \end{align*}
    Since in $\auc{A}_m$ agent $v$ is truthful, and besides for bidding $v$ there is no other bid in $\auc{A}_n$ that would give a different outcome compared to $\auc{A}_m$, this proves that also in $\auc{A}_n$ agent $v$ is truthful.
\end{itemize}

We move on to prove the claim. Let $\auc{A}_1,\dots,\auc{A}_{m-1},\auc{A}_{m+1},\dots,\auc{A}_n$ be  optimal auctions for horizons $1,\dots,m-1,m+1,\dots,n-1$ respectively, distribution $\mathcal{D}$, reward function $g$ and the matching over-time cost function. Due to Theorem~\ref{theorem:rental_as_auctions}, the rental mechanism $\mech{M}_m\coloneq\brackets{\auc{A}_1,\dots,\auc{A}_m}$ is an optimal $\brackets{m,\mathcal{D},g}$-rental mechanism, i.e. $R_m=\bb{E}\sqbr{\mech{M}_m}$. Define similarly $\mech{M}_n\coloneq\brackets{\auc{A}_1,\dots,\auc{A}_n}$.
